# PAM configuration for wdm — Fedora and derivatives.
#
# Install as /etc/pam.d/wdm. The rpm package does this; the Arch and Debian
# stacks live in pam.d-wdm and pam.d-wdm.debian.
#
# Same shape as the Arch stack, but Fedora has no system-login: the common
# stack is authselect's system-auth, and session work that only makes sense
# for a real login — pam_lastlog and friends — is split into postlogin, which
# every login-capable service is expected to include after system-auth.
# system-auth is what brings in pam_systemd, which creates the logind session
# and XDG_RUNTIME_DIR that wdm passes to the launched session; without it most
# desktops will not start. pam_loginuid is not in system-auth, so it is listed
# here the way sddm and gdm list it.
#
# The session stack is bracketed by pam_selinux close/open, as every
# login-capable Fedora service brackets its own. `close` runs first and resets
# any exec context inherited from wdm; `open` computes the context the user's
# session should run in and arms setexeccon(3) for the exec that follows, so it
# has to come before system-auth rather than after.
#
# pam_keyinit is listed after pam_selinux open and pam_namespace, which is where
# Fedora's own login and sshd stacks put it: the session keyring is created with
# the exec context already computed. Running it earlier is not known to break
# anything, but this file cannot be tested from the machine it is written on, so
# it follows the ordering the distribution ships rather than an improvised one.
#
# ponytail: the bracket is inert as wdm currently forks. Both pam_selinux open
# and pam_keyinit set *per-thread* state — the armed exec context and the joined
# session keyring — and wdm runs pam_open_session on its PAM thread while
# forking the session from the main one. fork(2) copies only the calling thread,
# so neither reaches the session: a confined login (staff_u, guest_u, xguest_u)
# is still unconfined and MCS separation does not apply, on a distribution that
# ships SELinux enforcing. The modules stay because they are correct for the day
# that changes. The upgrade path is either to fork the session from the PAM
# thread, or to re-apply setexeccon(3) and keyctl_join_session_keyring(3) in the
# forking thread after pam_open_session returns.

auth        substack    system-auth
-auth       optional    pam_gnome_keyring.so
-auth       optional    pam_kwallet5.so
auth        include     postlogin

account     required    pam_nologin.so
account     include     system-auth

password    include     system-auth

session     required    pam_selinux.so close
session     required    pam_loginuid.so
session     required    pam_selinux.so open
session     optional    pam_namespace.so
session     optional    pam_keyinit.so force revoke
session     include     system-auth
session     include     postlogin
-session    optional    pam_gnome_keyring.so auto_start
-session    optional    pam_kwallet5.so auto_start
